hello everyone I'm Dave Robinson chief  data scientist at data camp and I'm  gonna be sharing some advice on live  tweeting so when Jared challenged me to  give this talk about 40 minutes ago I  had started to think about what kind of  advice I might want to give to this  group about live tweeting and I'm able  to give not only advice but up-to-date  literature review about the best  practices for live tweeting so first the  advice is that Twitter is easier than it  looks  so usually people might be nervous you  might say I don't know what this Twitter  thing is what am I going to do with it  and then once you try it out you can  find it can be really fun really  rewarding and a great way to participate  in the conference with everyone else so  you just so I've had a lot of experience  live tweeting I found it a really great  way to keep track of what I've done in a  conference so for example in 2016 I went  to some conferences live tweeted them  and I was able to take those and  assemble them into a blog post that  makes me really happy that I was able to  keep track of these while I was going  and I'll share what I've learned first  always make sure you use the conference  hashtag so here that's hashtag our stats  NYC and that also lets you compete on  the leaderboards that they've been  showing off always at the speaker so  always include an act than the speakers  on screen I Twitter handle you'll notice  that in the program Jared has set up  that every speaker has their handle in  their description and always include a  picture this is a practice that I really  enjoy it's great especially great here  we you have these screens around you can  include a screen very close up of this  of the slide or you can include a screen  of the speaker and it's a really good  way to get people that aren't at the  conference to kind of feel like they're  there and it tends to get more  engagement they'll show some  quantitative evidence about that later  three things you could use to tweet  about one would be to introduce a person  or a topic so yesterday for example I  introduced when David Smith started  talking about Minecraft I tweeted to say  I'm really looking forward to this he's  combined are in Minecraft and give a  sense of what the talks about we can  spread one insight  talk like yesterday Noam Ross talked  about sharing data within our stats  package and I took a picture of that  slide and kind of passed those on so  people are that aren't at the conference  can understand a bit what he was  communicating or you can link to an  additional resource that the speaker  might want people to read this morning  Hadley gave a talk where he referred to  his chapter of advanced are on names and  values so I did a tweet that linked to  that and let's uh people that maybe are  either at the conference or following  along can understand a bit more of the  context around the talk and one last  piece of advice they have really seen  them done really well this year's show  off and by that I mean take something  you're talented at and use it to enhance  everyone's speak speaking understanding  so Brook Watson if you're not seeing  these tweets they're really fantastic  she's a talented artist and note taker  and she makes a little summary of each  combine with the profile of them and  they've been really fantastic way to  take something that she's talented at  and use it to enhance everyone's  experience to the conference so if  you're not following Brook Watson you  definitely should I just started  yesterday so that's some qualitative  advice but it's always important to take  a look at the literature in particular a  randomized experiment earlier I said  that it's better to include a picture  and gets more engagement but what does  the actual X I until a so to do that we  take a look at Robinson and Robinson  2018 where there were two tweets posted  it almost exactly the same time by two  tweeters where one of them didn't  include a picture and one of them did so  it turned out it but I went a bit  against the common consensus about  pictures because there was where 76  retweets on the one that didn't have a  picture and 64 on the one that did but  of course you can't just look at the  original experiment you need to look at  the following scholarly discourse in  this case we have a follow-up Robinson  2018 that pointed out that the original  did not use an appropriate normalization  metric and we should have normalized by  the number of followers it was followed  by a comment on Robinson 2018  along the lines of the fact that life  isn't fair and finally of course most  importantly we have peer review where  others that are involved in this space  are able to give their own opinion about  this discussion thank you  you
 

